  "provider_query": "Public Telegram web preview of the official Telegram channel",
  "answer": "The official Telegram channel’s public web preview is **https://t.me/s/telegram** (username `@telegram`). Anyone can open it in a browser without logging in.\n\nRelevant results:\n\n1. **https://t.me/s/telegram** — Public preview of **Telegram News** (`@telegram`): “The official Telegram on Telegram.” About 9–11M subscribers.  \n   Source: t.me channel preview pages.\n\n2. **https://t.me/telegram** — Standard public link; choose **Preview channel** to reach the same web view.  \n   Source: Telegram blog on public channel viewing.\n\n3. **https://telegram.org/blog/privacy-discussions-web-bots** — Official note that any public channel can be viewed on the web without an account; example given is `t.me/s/telegram`.  \n   Source: telegram.org blog.\n\n4. **https://telegram.org/tour/channels** — Channels tour: public channels are visible on the web and indexed; points to the official `@Telegram` channel for updates.  \n   Source: telegram.org.\n\n5. **https://telegram.org/faq_channels** — Channels FAQ; invites users to join the official Telegram channel for product updates; public channels use links like `t.me/telegram`.  \n   Source: telegram.org FAQ.\n\n6. **https://core.telegram.org/widgets/post** — How to embed posts from public channels (open a `t.me` post link and use **Embed**).  \n   Source: Telegram API docs.\n\n7. **https://t.me/s/TelegramTips** — Related official tips channel web preview (not the main news channel).  \n   Source: t.me preview.\n\n8. **https://telegram.org** — Main site and blog where official announcements are published (the channel itself warns that real announcements come from telegram.org).  \n   Source: telegram.org and channel posts.",
